opens100 provides CMake targets:

    find_package(opens100 CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE opens100::featurecatalog opens100::geometry)

Headers keep the module directory names:

    #include <FeatureCatalog/FeatureCatalogue.h>
    #include <GeoMetryLibrary/MBR.h>

The portrayal catalogue (feature "portrayal-catalogue") is opens100::portrayalcatalogue:

    #include <PortrayalCatalogue/PortrayalCatalogue.h>

The System Database (feature "system-database") is opens100::sdb:

    #include <SystemDatabase/SystemDatabase.h>

The dataset model and readers (feature "dataset") are opens100::dataset:

    #include <GISLibrary/S101Cell.h>

The Lua session of S-101 portrayal (feature "lua-scripting") is opens100::luascripting:

    #include <LuaScriptingReference/lua_session.h>

Running the portrayal rules over a dataset (feature "portrayal") is opens100::portrayal. It
leaves drawing instructions; a renderer of your own visits them:

    #include <GISLibrary/S100Layer.h>
    #include <GISLibrary/SENC_InstructionVisitor.h>

Drawing the instructions with Skia (feature "render-skia", under review against the Direct2D viewer)
is opens100::render-skia. The canvas is yours, and so are the font files; a surface in memory
with PNG output is included:

    #include <RenderSkia/SkiaRenderer.h>
    #include <RenderSkia/SkiaFonts.h>
    #include <RenderSkia/SkiaImage.h>

The same instructions as JSON text, identical on every platform (to compare two builds or
to feed another renderer):

    #include <GISLibrary/DisplayListJson.h>
